== A Word to the Reader

Dear friend!

Solomon bids us buy the#footnote[Prov 23:23.] truth, but does not tell us what it must cost, because we must get it however dear it may be. We must love it both#footnote[#emph[Multi amant veritatem lucentem, oderunt redarguentem.] \[Many love the truth when it shines, but hate it when it rebukes them.\]] shining and scorching. Every scrap of truth is precious, like the filings of gold; we must either live with it or die for it. As Ruth said to Naomi, “Wherever you go, I will go, and#footnote[Ruth 1:16, 17.] wherever you lodge, I will lodge, and nothing but death will part you and me,” so gracious spirits must say, “Where truth goes, I will go, and where truth lodges, I will#footnote[#emph[Si veritas est causa discordiae, mori possum tacere non possum.] \[If truth is the cause of discord, I can die, but I cannot keep silent.\] Jerome.] lodge, and nothing but death will part me and truth.” A man may lawfully sell his house, lands, and jewels, but truth is a jewel that exceeds all price, and must not be sold. It is our heritage: “Your testimonies are my heritage#footnote[Ps 119:111.] forever.” It is a legacy that our forefathers bought with their blood, which should make us willing to lay down anything and to lay out anything, so that, with the wise merchant in the gospel, we may purchase this precious pearl,#footnote[Matt 5:18. #emph[Veritas vincit.] Truth at last triumphs.] which is worth more than heaven and earth, and which will make a man live happily, die comfortably, and reign eternally.

And now, if you please, read the work, and take this counsel from me.

First, you must know that a man may be useful even though he cannot be excellent. An iron key may unlock the door of a golden treasure; indeed (#emph[ferrum potest quod aurum non potest]) iron can do some things that gold cannot, etc.

Secondly, remember that it is not hasty reading, but serious meditating on#footnote[It is a law among the Parsees in India to think beforehand about what they are going to do: if it is bad, to reject it; if good, to do it.] holy and heavenly truths, that makes them prove sweet and profitable to the soul. It is not the bee’s touching of the flower that gathers honey, but her staying a while on the flower that draws out the sweetness. It is not he who reads most, but he who meditates most, who will prove the choicest, sweetest, wisest, and strongest Christian, etc.

Thirdly, know that it is not the knowing man, nor the talking man, nor the reading man, but the doing man, who in the end will be found the happiest man. “If you#super[+] know these things, you#super[+] will be blessed#footnote[John 13:17. Matt 7:21.] and happy if you#super[+] do them.” “Not everyone who says to Me, ‘Lord, Lord,’ will enter the#footnote[It was a good saying of Justin Martyr, #emph[Non in verbis sed in factis res nostrae religionis consistunt.] \[The substance of our religion lies not in words but in deeds.\]] kingdom of heaven, but only he who does the will of My Father in heaven.” Judas called Christ Lord, Lord, and yet betrayed him, and has gone to his own place. Ah, how many Judases we have in these days, who kiss Christ and yet betray Christ; who profess him in their words but deny him in their works; who bow#footnote[The heathen philosopher (Seneca) did not like those who are (#emph[semper victuri]) always about to live better, but never begin. God loves (says Luther) #emph[curristas,] not #emph[quaeristas,] the runner, not the questioner. Pacuvius has a fine saying: “I hate,” he says, “the men who are idle in deed and philosophical in word,” etc.] their knee to him and yet despise him in their hearts; who call him Jesus and yet will not obey him as their Lord!

#emph[Reader,] if it is not strong on your heart to practice what you read, to what end do you read? To increase your own condemnation? If your light and knowledge are not turned into practice, the more knowing a man you are, the more miserable a man you will be in the day of recompense; your light and knowledge will torment you more than all the devils in hell. Your knowledge will be the rod that will eternally lash you, and the scorpion that will forever bite you, and the worm that will everlastingly gnaw you. Therefore read, and labor to know, so that you may do, or else you are undone forever. When Demosthenes was asked what was the first part of an orator, what the second, and what the third, he answered, “Action.” I may say the same: if anyone should ask me what is the first, the second, the third part of a Christian, I must answer, “Action.” For the man who reads so that he may know, and labors to know so that he may do, will have two heavens: a heaven of joy, peace, and comfort on earth, and a heaven of glory and happiness after death.

Fourthly, and lastly, if in your reading you will cast a serious eye on the notes, you will find many sweet and precious notes that will often give light to the things you read, and repay your pains with much comfort and profit. So, desiring that you may find as much sweetness and benefit in reading this treatise as I have found (by the overshadowings of heaven) in studying and writing it, “I commit you to God and to the word of His grace, which can#footnote[Acts 20:32.] build you up and give you an inheritance among all who are sanctified.” And I remain,

Reader,

#v(1em)

#align(right)[Your soul’s servant in every office of the gospel,]

#align(right)[THOMAS BROOKS.]
